; The format is in Tutorials.md: four beats per key -- say the key with the
; word it comes from, press it, hear the reader word for word, say what that
; meant. Name silence. Name no screen reader. Twelve walks, each three to five
; minutes, in the Homer pattern the kit itself prescribes. Where the kit has
; no screen of its own, an app built on it -- DbDo, EdSharp, FileDir -- shows
; what the kit's code makes a person hear.

FileTask = tutorial

[feed]
Title=HomerDev Walkthroughs
Description=Spoken walks through the Homer Development Kit: what it is, how it builds and releases an app, and what its shared code makes a person hear. Twelve walks, each three to five minutes.
Author=Jamal Mazrui
Email=jamal.mazrui@outlook.com
Base=

[about]
Title=00 - Overview and Table of Contents
Intro=What the Homer Development Kit is, in a paragraph; the two reader keys every walk assumes; then the table of contents, one line per walk.
Setup=Nothing is needed; this walk is listened to.
Homework=Listen to the walks in order; each one assumes the ones before it.

[step]

Say=HomerDev is a kit of parts for building Windows programs that work well by keyboard and screen reader: shared C sharp classes and Python modules, build and release scripts, installer parts, templates for a new app, and skills that teach an AI assistant the house rules. Every decision that makes a program pleasant to use without sight -- where the focus goes, what is spoken, what the keys do -- has been made once and put in a class, so an app built on the kit inherits it. DbDo, EdSharp and FileDir are built on it, and these walks use their voices to show what the kit's code does for a person.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Two keys before anything else, both the reader's own. If a line goes by too fast, Insert plus Up Arrow says it again.
Key=Insert+UpArrow
Hear=(the last line, read a second time)
Say=Nothing changed; the key only repeats.

[step]

Say=And if you lose your place, Insert plus Tab says where you are: the control, its state, its position, and any hint it carries. The walks are heard with those hints off, the way most people work.
Key=Insert+Tab
Hear=(the current control, with its state and position)

[step]

Say=Now the table of contents. I say the number and the title; the reader says what the walk covers.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=One, Install and Launch.
Key=
Hear=Unarchiving the kit, running its own build, and launching the sample program it builds.

[step]

Say=Two, User Interface Concepts.
Key=
Hear=What a Homer program is made of, heard in DbDo, EdSharp and FileDir: the Lbc dialog, the Say layer, the status line, the single instance.

[step]

Say=Three, Key Patterns.
Key=
Hear=The rules every Homer key follows, and the same key heard in three programs.

[step]

Say=Four, Start an App from the Kit.
Key=
Hear=A new app from the template: its files, its first build, its first run.

[step]

Say=Five, Build, Check and Release.
Key=
Hear=What build does, stage by stage; what check refuses; what release publishes.

[step]

Say=Six, The Installer and Its Finish Page.
Key=
Hear=The shared installer parts: boxes that say their state, the results box, the summary.

[step]

Say=Seven, Spoken Tutorials.
Key=
Hear=Writing a walk, the two voices, the pattern of twelve, and the tool that speaks and measures them.

[step]

Say=Eight, Shared Code and the Homer Player.
Key=
Hear=The classes every app uses, and the player heard in FileDir and DbDo alike.

[step]

Say=Nine, Glossary.
Key=
Hear=The words the kit uses, in alphabetical order, one line each.

[step]

Say=Ten, Conclusion.
Key=
Hear=Four sentences to carry away, one thing from each walk, and where to begin.

[step]

Say=Eleven, More Information.
Key=
Hear=The guide, the history, the skills, the GitHub page, and the apps built on the kit.

[step]

Say=Twelve walks, three to five minutes each, about an hour together. They are a course, not a reference: each one assumes those before it.
Key=
Hear=
